missing numbers in c001..c145: [31, 77]
FC2-c051 L04 None | What are the payoffs of a call and a put at expiry, with strike K? | The call pays (S_τ − K)^+ and the put pays (K − S_τ)^+, where x^+ = max(x, 0) (Definition 1.1). The call is in the money when S_t > K, and the put when S_t < K. | 
FC2-c052 L04 T | What does the payoff identity (s − K)^+ − (K − s)^+ equal? | s − K for every real s (Lemma 1.2). A long call and a short put with the same strike and expiry therefore pay S_τ − K, the payoff of a long forward (Remark 1.3). | 
FC2-c053 L04 None | What is the difference between American and European exercise? | An American option may be exercised at any date t in [0, τ] for its intrinsic value h(S_t). A European option may be exercised only at expiry τ, as A5 states (Definition 2.1). | 
FC2-c054 L04 T | Under A2 and A3, how does the price of an American option compare with its intrinsic value? | V_A(t) ≥ h(S_t) at every date t (Proposition 2.2). | 
FC2-c055 L04 T | Under A2, A3 and A5, is an American option at date 0 worth at least the European option with the same terms? | Yes: V_A(0) ≥ V_E(0) (Proposition 2.3). The proof gives only this inequality, and Remark 2.4 does not claim that it is strict. | 
FC2-c056 L04 T | On the SIMULATED path 100, 120, 90, 80, 110 USD, with strike 100 USD, expiry after four years and a one-year discount factor of 9/10, what is the date-0 value of holding the call to expiry? | 10(9/10)^4 = 6.561 USD, which is 6.56 USD to the cent (Example 2.5; Lab 1, Part 2). | Example 2.5; Lab 1, Part 2 (SIMULATED path)
FC2-c057 L04 T | On the Example 2.5 path, what is the put's best exercise date, and what is it worth at date 0? | Date 3, worth 20(9/10)^3 = 14.58 USD at date 0. Holding the put to expiry is worth 0 on this path (Example 2.5; Lab 1, Part 2). | Example 2.5; Lab 1, Part 2 (SIMULATED path)
FC2-c058 L04 T | What are the call and put break-even spots at expiry for the premiums of Lab 1? | Call 108 USD, the strike plus the 8 USD premium. Put 93 USD, the strike minus the 7 USD premium (Lab 1, Part 1). | Lab 1, Part 1 (exact)
FC2-c059 L04 T | Is an American option always worth more than its European counterpart? | No. Proposition 2.3 proves only the weak inequality V_A(0) ≥ V_E(0), and the lesson does not claim that it is strict for any put or call (Remark 2.4). | 
FC2-c060 L04 T | Should a call that is in the money be exercised at once? | Not on this lesson's argument. Exercise gives S_t − K, while selling the option gives its market value, which is at least S_t − K by Proposition 2.2. Exercising at once is therefore never better than selling at the market price. | 
FC2-c061 L04 T | Is the payoff at expiry the profit? | No. The profit subtracts the premium. In Lab 1 the call pays 25.00 USD at a spot of 125 USD, but its profit is 17.00 USD after the 8.00 USD premium (Lab 1, Part 1). | Lab 1, Part 1 (exact)
FC2-c062 L04 T | Is the best exercise date on a price path a trading rule? | No. Example 2.5 picks the best date after the path is known. A rule must choose the date from the prices observed so far (Remark 2.6). | 
FC2-c063 L05 None | What is the put-call parity identity for European options with strike K and expiry τ? | C − P = S − DK, with D = e^{−rτ} the discount factor (Definition 1.1). | 
FC2-c064 L05 T | Under A1 to A5, does put-call parity hold for every strike? | Yes: C − P = S − e^{−rτ}K for every strike K > 0 (Theorem 1.2). The proof uses the four payoffs, A1 and A2 (no income, free short sales and lending), A3, and the rate of A4. It uses no distribution for S_τ (Remark 1.3). | 
FC2-c065 L05 None | What are the model-free option bounds? | Inequalities that European call and put prices must satisfy under A1 to A5 and (N), where (N) says the spot is non-negative at expiry. They use no model of how the spot is distributed (Definition 2.1). | 
FC2-c066 L05 T | Under A1 to A5 and (N), what bounds do European call and put prices satisfy? | 0 ≤ C ≤ S; C ≥ max(S − DK, 0); 0 ≤ P ≤ DK; P ≥ max(DK − S, 0) (Theorem 2.2). | 
FC2-c067 L05 T | Which of the four model-free bounds use (N)? | Parts (a) and (c) use (N), because min(S_τ, K) is non-negative only when S_τ ≥ 0. Parts (b) and (d) do not use (N) (Remark 2.3). | 
FC2-c068 L05 None | In this lesson, what is convexity in strike, and what is the weight λ? | For strikes K1 < K2 < K3, with λ = (K3 − K2)/(K3 − K1), call prices satisfy convexity in strike at the triple if C(K2) ≤ λ C(K1) + (1 − λ) C(K3) (Definition 3.1). The word refers to strikes only. | 
FC2-c069 L05 T | Under A2, A3, A5 and (N), are European call prices convex in strike? | Yes, at every triple K1 < K2 < K3 with the same spot and expiry (Theorem 3.2). The proof is a payoff argument on the butterfly position, checked in four cases. | 
FC2-c070 L05 T | Under A2, A3, A5 and (N), do call prices rise with strike? | No, they do not rise: C(K2) ≤ C(K1) whenever K1 < K2 (Corollary 3.4). The corollary gives no size for the fall. | 
FC2-c071 L05 T | Under A1 to A5, (N) and r ≥ 0, what is the date-0 price of an American call on an underlying with no income? | The same as the European call: V_A(0) = V_E(0) (Theorem 4.1). The theorem is proved at date 0 only. The equality V_A(t) = V_E(t) at a later date t is a remark, not a proved result (Remark 4.2). | 
FC2-c072 L05 C | On the synthetic price set of Lab 1, what do the parity and bound checks print? | The parity cost is zero at each of the five strikes, 80 to 120 USD. The parity payoff is zero at all 40 (strike, expiry spot) pairs, and no model-free bound or convexity triple is violated (Lab 1). | Lab 1 (SIMULATED price set; exact integer arithmetic)
FC2-c073 L05 C | What does a put raised by 300 cents at 100 USD do to the parity cost and to the payoff check in Lab 1? | The parity cost at that strike shows −300 cents. The payoff check finds nothing, because the payoff at expiry does not depend on the put price (Lab 1; Research R1). | Lab 1 (planted error; SIMULATED price set)
FC2-c074 L05 C | When a call and a put at 90 USD are both raised by 500 cents in Lab 1, does put-call parity still hold? | Yes. The parity cost stays zero at every strike, because the call and the put rise by the same amount (Lab 1). | Lab 1 (planted error; SIMULATED price set)
FC2-c075 L05 T | What are the evenly spaced second differences of the Lab 1 calls at 90, 100 and 110 USD? | 0, 100 and 800 cents. All three are non-negative as Theorem 3.2 requires, and the one at 90 USD is zero (Problem E1). | Problem E1 (exact)
FC2-c076 L05 T | Does the early-exercise bound for a put show that holding a put is worth at least its exercise value? | No. With K = 100, S_t = 50, one year left and r = 0.10, the put's bound is 100e^{−0.1} − 50 = 40.48 USD, below the 50 USD that exercise pays now (Section 5). | Section 5 (arithmetic on the page)
FC2-c078 L05 T | Is a call priced above the stock a bargain to buy? | No. C ≤ S (Theorem 2.2(a)). Selling the call and buying the share has initial value S − C < 0 and a non-negative payoff, which A3 excludes. | 
FC2-c079 L05 C | Does a convexity violation show that one call is mispriced on its own? | No. Convexity in strike is a condition on a triple of strikes. In Lab 1 the planted error at 90 USD produces three violated triples, all sharing the same raised call (Lab 1). | Lab 1 (planted error; SIMULATED price set)
FC2-c080 L05 T | Does Theorem 4.1 still give equal American and European call prices when the rate r is negative? | The theorem makes no claim for r < 0. With r < 0 the bound in Theorem 2.2(b) can fall below S_t − K, so the step of the proof that uses r ≥ 0 fails (Remark 4.2; Problem R2). | 
FC2-c081 L06 None | What are the gross riskless return and the value today of a sure payoff of 1 at the end of the period? | R_f = 1 + r_1, where r_1 is the one-period rate, and a sure payoff of 1 is worth 1/R_f today (Definition 1.1). The unsubscripted r of A4 is not used in this lesson. | 
FC2-c082 L06 None | What is the one-period binomial model, and what does A6 require? | The stock is worth S_0 today and uS_0 or dS_0 at the end of the period. A6 requires 0 < d < R_f < u. The call pays max(uS_0 − K, 0) after an up move and max(dS_0 − K, 0) after a down move (Definition 1.2). | 
FC2-c083 L06 None | What does a replicating portfolio hold, and what equations does it satisfy? | Δ shares and a bond position B, where B > 0 is cash lent and B < 0 is cash borrowed. It satisfies Δ uS_0 + R_f B = V_1(u) and Δ dS_0 + R_f B = V_1(d). Its cost today is V_0 = Δ S_0 + B (Definition 1.3). | 
FC2-c084 L06 T | Under A1, A2, A3, A5 and A6, is the one-period replicating portfolio unique, and what is the price? | It is unique, because u ≠ d and S_0 > 0. Its cost is V_0 = (q V_1(u) + (1 − q) V_1(d)) / R_f with q = (R_f − d)/(u − d), and 0 < q < 1 (Theorem 1.4(a) and (b)). | 
FC2-c085 L06 T | In the one-period binomial model, what happens if a claim trades at a price π different from the replicating cost V_0? | The price is not the replicating cost, so a zero-cost portfolio with a strictly positive payoff in every state exists, which A3 excludes; under A3 the price is V_0. If π > V_0, sell the claim, buy the replicating portfolio and lend the surplus; the total is (π − V_0) R_f > 0 in both states at zero cost. If π < V_0, reverse the positions (Theorem 1.4(c); Example 1.6). | 
FC2-c086 L06 T | In Example 1.5 (S_0 = K = 100, u = 3/2, d = 1/2, R_f = 5/4), what are the replicating shares and the bond position? | Δ = 1/2 share and B = −20 (borrowing 20) (Example 1.5). | Example 1.5; Lab 1 (exact)
FC2-c087 L06 T | Does the real-world probability of an up move enter the one-period price? | No. The formula in Theorem 1.4(b) uses u, d, R_f and the two payoffs. Two models with the same u, d and R_f but different up probabilities give the same V_0 (Remark 1.7). | 
FC2-c088 L06 None | What is the risk-neutral probability of an up move in the one-period binomial model? | q = (R_f − d)/(u − d), with Q(up) = q and Q(down) = 1 − q. Expectations under Q are written E^Q (Definition 2.1). | 
FC2-c089 L06 T | Under the hypotheses of Theorem 1.4, how is the one-period price written as an expectation? | V_0 = E^Q[V_1] / R_f, the discounted expectation of the payoff under Q (Theorem 2.2). | 
FC2-c090 L06 None | What is a recombining tree? | A tree in which an up move followed by a down move reaches the same node as a down move followed by an up move. Node (n, k) has stock price S(n, k) = S_0 u^k d^{n−k}, so there are n + 1 nodes at time n, not 2^n (Definition 3.1). | 
FC2-c091 L06 T | Is the risk-neutral probability q the probability that the stock goes up? | No. In Example 1.5, q = 3/4, while the stock's up factor is u = 3/2. q is the weight that makes the discounted expected payoff equal the replicating cost (Theorem 2.2; Definition 2.1). | Example 1.5
FC2-c092 L06 None | What is backward induction on the recombining tree? | Set V(N, k) = f(S(N, k)) at expiry, and for n < N set V(n, k) = (q V(n+1, k+1) + (1 − q) V(n+1, k)) / R_f, with S(n, k) = S_0 u^k d^{n−k} (Definitions 3.1 and 3.2). | 
FC2-c093 L06 T | Under A1, A2, A3, A5, A6 and A6′, what is the multi-period price, and what is the closed form at the root? | The price at node (n, k) is V(n, k) (Theorem 3.3). At the root, V(0,0) = R_f^{−N} Σ_k C(N, k) q^k (1 − q)^{N−k} f(S_0 u^k d^{N−k}). | 
FC2-c094 L06 T | What is the two-period call price in Example 3.4 (the parameters of Example 1.5)? | V(0,0) = 45 currency units, that is 4500 cents at date 0 (Example 3.4; Lab 1 prints 45). | Example 3.4; Lab 1 (exact)
FC2-c095 L06 T | Does the self-financing strategy built from the time-0 holdings reproduce the claim on every path in Lab 1? | Yes. Lab 1 prints True for the paths uu, ud, du and dd. The time-0 holdings are 3/4 share and a bond position of −30, at cost 45 (Lab 1; Lab 1 checks each node value). | Lab 1 (exact)
FC2-c096 L06 T | With next prices 150, 100 and 50 and only a stock and a bond to trade, can the payoff (1, 0, 0) be replicated? | No. The last two equations force Δ = 0 and R_f B = 0, and then the first equation reads 0 = 1 (Section 4). Replication depends on the payoff vector, not only on the number of states. | Section 4 (on the page)
FC2-c097 L06 T | In Example 1.5, if the call trades at 35, what does selling it, buying 1/2 share and borrowing 20 leave today, and what does the position pay at expiry? | Cash of 35 − 50 + 20 = 5 today, lent at R_f = 5/4, so it pays 25/4 at expiry. With the sold call, the position pays 25/4 in both states at zero cost (Example 1.6). | Example 1.6
FC2-c098 L06 T | Is the call's price the payoff at the expected final stock price, discounted? | No. The expected final price discounts to S_0 = 100, and the payoff at that price is 56.25, which discounts to 36. The correct price is 45, the discounted expectation of the payoff (Theorem 3.3; Example 3.4). | Example 3.4 (arithmetic on the page)
FC2-c132 L05 T | Does Lesson 5 give a rule for exercising a deep in-the-money call early when r ≥ 0? | No. Under A1 to A5, (N) and r ≥ 0, Theorem 4.1 gives equal American and European call prices at date 0 only. The lesson proves no rule for holding against exercising at a later date (Remark 4.2). | 
FC2-c135 L04 T | On the Example 2.5 path, what is the date-0 value of exercising the call at date 1? | 20 · 9/10 = 18.00 USD, the largest discounted intrinsic value on the path (Example 2.5; Lab 1, Part 2). | Example 2.5; Lab 1, Part 2 (SIMULATED path)
FC2-c136 L06 T | In Example 1.5, what is the cost of the replicating portfolio, in currency units? | V_0 = ΔS_0 + B = 50 − 20 = 30 currency units, which is 3000 cents and also (3/4 · 50)/(5/4) (Example 1.5). | Example 1.5; Lab 1 (exact)
FC2-c137 L06 T | In Example 3.4, what is the value after one up move, V(1,1), in currency units? | 75 currency units, from (3/4 · 125)/(5/4) = 75 (Example 3.4; Lab 1 prints 75). | Example 3.4; Lab 1 (exact)
FC2-c142 L04 None | Under Assumption A3 of Lesson 4, what must a portfolio with initial value V_0 ≤ 0 and a non-negative payoff satisfy? | Its initial value and its payoff must both be zero: V_0 = 0 and X = 0 in every state. A3 is a hypothesis about markets, used in the proofs. | 
FC2-c143 L04 None | Under Assumption A4 of Lesson 4, what is the value at date τ of an amount x held from date t? | x e^{r(τ − t)}, since the rate r is constant over the life of the contract and compounded continuously (A4 of Lesson 4). | 
FC2-c145 L05 T | Does put-call parity hold only for at-the-money options? | No. Theorem 1.2 holds for every strike K > 0, and its proof uses no property of the strike (Theorem 1.2). | 
